From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: TheDBP <TheDBP@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 19:56:09 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] msm8996-common: ims: pick the carrier profile from the SIM so
 REGISTER can do sec-agree

The IMS profile provisioned into lgims.db decides whether the SIP REGISTER performs the RFC 3329
security agreement, and stock picks that profile from the handset model: com.lge.os.Build.CA_TARGET
is VZW/US on a vs995, so a Verizon profile is provisioned whatever SIM is present. That profile
sets aos_reg_0_ipsec=false, the REGISTER carries no Security-Client, and any P-CSCF requiring
sec-agree answers 421 Extension Required. Registration then blocks, because
VZWAoSRegistration::ProcessStartFailed_StatusCode classifies 421 as non-retryable in a hardcoded
bitmask that no provisioned retry policy can reach.

The profile matching the SIM's own network sets aos_reg_0_ipsec=true. Verified on hardware with a
T-Mobile SIM: Security-Client offered, 401 with Security-Server instead of 421, AKA answered by the
ISIM, four SAs and six SPs installed through ipsecstarter and ipsecclient, protected re-REGISTER
answered 200 OK, STATE_REGISTERED and IMS_VOLTE_INDICATOR_ON.

Selection uses the stock override rather than new app code: OperatorInfo reads
persist.lg.ims.pref_operator and persist.lg.ims.pref_country in its static initialiser and prefers
them over the model target. A oneshot started from gsm.sim.operator.numeric maps the MCCMNC through
ims-operator-map.txt and sets them. The non-US entries come from the stock app's own mccmnc_list,
filtered to operators that ship a profile; that table has no US coverage because LG pins US carriers
to the model, so US entries are added. An unmapped SIM leaves both properties unset and the stock
target profile applies, so only mapped codes change behaviour.

The properties persist, so a boot that reads the SIM after the app has initialised still leaves the
correct value for the next one. Verified on device that the mapping resolves, that a second run is a
no-op rather than forcing a needless re-provision, and that an unmapped code leaves the properties
untouched.

